																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13930238/love-at-long-last">Love at Long Last</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4843982">
									

<blockquote>
<p>This isn't my original story: I received the idea from this youtube video&#8230;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There's a fine line between inspiration and plagiarism&#8212;all writers get inspiration from other stories and there's nothing truly original under the sun, but the new piece has to set itself apart from the source it's drawing from. Whether or not it succeeds at that is usually very subjective.</p>
<p>I watched the video. It's a <em>very</em> good story. My concern is that you run the risk of hewing too closely to the original writing while still changing enough plot elements to take the impact out of. <em>Dear Nia</em> works so well not because of the global stakes with the destruction of Soren's Earth, but the emotional conflict that arises from two opposing points of view. In my opinion, that feels much more emotionally complex (and therefore more resonant) than the basic &quot;Desire &gt; Recklessness &gt; Regret&quot; arc you've outlined above.</p>
<p>The ending sounds pretty flat, what with one character vanishing with no explanation and the other one ending up in Foundation custody to act as an exposition machine. Consider what other outcomes may arise for them. I like the idea of an RMK-Class Scenario getting thrown into the mix; to me that's the most intriguing part of your idea and potentially its greatest strength, so I recommend you focus on developing that aspect of the plot and its ramifications on both the world and its characters.</p>

							</div>

								
					
